<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Phimai regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1767 CE – 1768 CE · capital Phimai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Siamese breakaway regime on the Khorat plateau after Ayutthaya's fall, led by a prince of the old royal house.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> fell to the Burmese in 1767, its provincial governors and princes set up rival regimes. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Khorat plateau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thepphiphit">Prince Thepphiphit</a>, a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommakot">Borommakot</a> who had earlier been exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, gathered followers and claimed authority as the legitimate heir.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime controlled the north-eastern plateau around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a>, a region with old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> temples and Lao and Khmer populations. Its power rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thepphiphit">Thepphiphit's</a> royal prestige and local nobles. It was one of five main contenders for the Siamese succession.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1768 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> and defeated its forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thepphiphit">Thepphiphit</a> was captured and executed, and the plateau was brought under Thonburi rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1767 CE – 1768 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.87%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1767 CE – 1768 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phimai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thepphiphit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thai, Lao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chao Phimai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=phimai-regime" aria-label="Phimai regime on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7479 1611 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Phimai regime lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7799 1867L7752 1900M7799 1867L7753 1923"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7752 1900h0M7753 1923h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7799 1867h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7799 1867h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phimai. <a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=phimai-regime">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
